{"id": "valpaca0004", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.16, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1618, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": null, "query": "How do I wrap a present neatly?", "transcript": "How do I wrap a present neatly?", "mode": "local", "answer": "To wrap a present neatly start by placing the gift in the center of the wrapping paper. Fold one side over the gift and crease it sharply with your fingernail or a bone folder. Do the same for the other sides, tucking in any edges as you go. For the top fold it down and then fold the bottom up meeting the sides. Smooth out any wrinkles and trim excess paper if needed. Finish with a bow or ribbon for a polished look!", "answer_ms": 3125}
{"id": "valpaca0007", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 1.51, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.197, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Who is Larry Page?", "transcript": "Who is Larry Page?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Larry Page is one of the co-founders of Google.", "answer_ms": 426}
{"id": "valpaca0010", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 5.36, "n_chunks": 6, "n_concurrent": 6, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3039, "eot_read_ms": 35, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Do you think retinoid is effective on removing the acne? Because I have a lot of it.", "transcript": "Do you think retinoid is effective on removing the acne? Because I have a lot of it.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Yes—topical retinoids are often effective for acne, especially clogged pores and preventing new breakouts. It usually takes a few weeks to see results, so be patient! Just remember to use it as directed, wear sunscreen daily, and moisturize if your skin gets dry. If you have a lot of acne or it’s not improving, I’d recommend talking to a dermatologist about other treatment options.", "expert_answer": "Yes—topical retinoids (like adapalene, tretinoin, or tazarotene) are often effective for acne, especially clogged pores/blackheads/whiteheads and preventing new breakouts. They can also help with acne marks over time.\n\nKey points:\n- Results usually take 8–12 weeks, and acne may look worse at first (“purging”).\n- Start slowly: a pea-sized amount for the whole face, 2–3 nights/week, then increase as tolerated.\n- Use moisturizer and sunscreen; retinoids can cause dryness, peeling, and sun sensitivity.\n- Avoid if pregnant or trying to become pregnant unless your clinician says it’s safe.\n\nBecause you have “a lot” of acne, it’s worth seeing a dermatologist or healthcare provider—moderate/severe acne may need combination treatment, such as benzoyl peroxide, antibiotics, hormonal therapy, or isotretinoin. If you have painful cysts, scarring, or acne affecting your confidence, don’t wait to get help.", "expert_latency_s": 12.18, "stall_ms": 49, "relay_ms": 2854}
{"id": "valpaca0013", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.81, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1248, "eot_read_ms": 31, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What are the best exercises for beginners?", "transcript": "What are the best exercises for beginners?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The best exercises for beginners are those that are gentle on the body, easy to learn, and can be done with little or no equipment. Walking is a great place to start because it's low impact and improves cardiovascular health. Yoga helps with flexibility, balance, and relaxation, while bodyweight exercises like squats, push-ups, and lunges build strength without needing weights. Swimming is also excellent because it works multiple muscle groups and is easy on the joints. The key is to start slowly, listen to your body, and gradually increase intensity as you build fitness and confidence.", "answer_ms": 4057}
{"id": "valpaca0016", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.81, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4657, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Are there any weird Christmas traditions?", "transcript": "Are there any weird Christmas traditions?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Yes—many countries have Christmas traditions that seem unusual or “weird” from the outside. A few examples include Krampus in parts of Europe, where a horned creature accompanies St. Nicholas; Iceland’s Yule Cat that supposedly eats people who don’t get new clothes; and Japan’s tradition of eating Kentucky Fried Chicken on Christmas. There are also quirky customs like the decorated pooping log in Spain or small figurines placed in nativity scenes to represent good luck. So yes, Christmas can be pretty delightfully strange depending on where you are!", "expert_answer": "Yes—many countries have Christmas traditions that seem unusual or “weird” from the outside. A few examples:\n\n- **Krampus (Austria/Germany/Central Europe):** A horned creature who accompanies St. Nicholas and scares or punishes naughty children.\n- **The Yule Cat (Iceland):** A giant Christmas cat said to eat people who don’t receive new clothes before Christmas.\n- **KFC Christmas Dinner (Japan):** Eating Kentucky Fried Chicken on Christmas has become a popular holiday tradition.\n- **Caga Tió (Catalonia, Spain):** A decorated “pooping log” that children hit with sticks while singing, until it “produces” treats.\n- **Caganer (Catalonia):** A small figurine of a person pooping, placed in nativity scenes as a symbol of fertility or good luck.\n- **La Befana (Italy):** A kindly witch delivers gifts to children on Epiphany, rather than Santa on Christmas Eve.\n- **Spider Web Decorations (Ukraine):** Christmas trees may be decorated with spider webs, linked to a folk tale about good fortune.\n- **Roller-skating to Mass (Venezuela):** In Caracas, some people traditionally roller-skate to early Christmas Mass.\n\nSo yes—Christmas traditions can get pretty delightfully strange depending on where you are.", "expert_latency_s": 8.39, "stall_ms": 38, "relay_ms": 4013}
{"id": "valpaca0019", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 9.14, "n_chunks": 10, "n_concurrent": 10, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0661, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": null, "query": "I am going to try to roast a pig at home for Thanksgiving this year. What equipment and techniques do I need to successfully get a pig roasted?", "transcript": "I am going to try to roast a pig at home for Thanksgiving this year. What equipment and techniques do I need to successfully get a pig roasted?", "mode": "local", "answer": "To roast a pig successfully, you'll need some key equipment and techniques. First, get a large roasting spit or rotisserie rod that can support the weight of the pig. You'll also need a sturdy outdoor grill or rotisserie setup capable of holding the spit securely. A heavy-duty thermometer is essential to monitor the internal temperature—pork should reach about 145°F for safety. Use a strong binding material like twine or skewers to secure the legs in place on the spit. For even cooking, make sure the heat source is consistent and protected from wind if outdoors. Season the pig generously with salt, pepper, and herbs before placing it on the spit. Rotate the pig slowly as it cooks to ensure even browning. Finally, consider using a drip pan underneath to catch excess fat and keep things tidy. With these tools and techniques, your Thanksgiving pig roast is sure to be a delicious centerpiece!", "answer_ms": 6969}
{"id": "valpaca0022", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.92, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3063, "eot_read_ms": 30, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What type of soil is suitable for cactus?", "transcript": "What type of soil is suitable for cactus?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Cacti need **well-draining, sandy or gritty soil**. A suitable mix is **cactus/succulent potting mix**, often containing **sand, perlite, pumice, or gravel**, so water drains quickly and the roots don’t stay wet.", "expert_answer": "Cacti need **well-draining, sandy or gritty soil**. A suitable mix is **cactus/succulent potting mix**, often containing **sand, perlite, pumice, or gravel**, so water drains quickly and the roots don’t stay wet.", "expert_latency_s": 2.35, "stall_ms": 41, "relay_ms": 1818}
{"id": "valpaca0025", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 3.31, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2292, "eot_read_ms": 33, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What are some species of bears that are now extinct?", "transcript": "What are some species of bears that are now extinct?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Some extinct bear species include the cave bear, giant short-faced bear, and several other ancient bears that lived during the Ice Age.", "expert_answer": "Some extinct bear species include:\n\n- **Cave bear** (*Ursus spelaeus*) — lived in Europe and Asia during the Pleistocene.\n- **Giant short-faced bear** (*Arctodus simus*) — lived in North America; one of the largest known bears.\n- **Lesser short-faced bear** (*Arctodus pristinus*) — a smaller North American short-faced bear.\n- **South American giant short-faced bear** (*Arctotherium angustidens*) — one of the largest bear species ever known.\n- **Florida spectacled bear** (*Tremarctos floridanus*) — lived in North America and was related to the modern spectacled bear.\n- **Etruscan bear** (*Ursus etruscus*) — an early bear species from Europe and Asia.\n- **Deninger’s bear** (*Ursus deningeri*) — likely an ancestor or close relative of the cave bear.\n- **Steppe brown bear** (*Ursus arctos priscus*) — an extinct subspecies of brown bear from the Ice Age.", "expert_latency_s": 5.62, "stall_ms": 38, "relay_ms": 1009}
{"id": "valpaca0028", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.92, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2009, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Why is Kobe beef so damn expensive?", "transcript": "Why is Kobe beef so damn expensive?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Kobe beef is so expensive mainly because it’s **rare, tightly regulated, and costly to produce**. It must come from Tajima-gyu cattle raised in Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan, under strict rules. Only carcasses meeting specific standards for lineage, marbling, weight, meat quality, and yield can be certified as Kobe beef. The cattle are raised carefully and slowly, with high feed and labor costs. Supply is limited—only a small number of cattle qualify each year—and global demand is huge, giving it a luxury reputation. Also, real Kobe beef is often served in small portions because it’s extremely rich and highly marbled. A lot of beef marketed loosely as “Kobe-style” outside Japan is not genuine certified Kobe beef.", "expert_answer": "Kobe beef is so expensive mainly because it’s **rare, tightly regulated, and costly to produce**.\n\n- **It must come from Tajima-gyu cattle** raised in Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan, under strict rules.\n- Only carcasses meeting specific standards for **lineage, marbling, weight, meat quality, and yield** can be certified as Kobe beef.\n- The cattle are raised carefully and slowly, with high feed and labor costs.\n- Supply is limited: only a small number of cattle qualify each year.\n- Global demand is huge, and “Kobe” has a luxury reputation.\n\nAlso, real Kobe beef is often served in small portions because it’s extremely rich and highly marbled. A lot of beef marketed loosely as “Kobe-style” outside Japan is not genuine certified Kobe beef.", "expert_latency_s": 4.42, "stall_ms": 34, "relay_ms": 5786}
{"id": "valpaca0031", "pool": "valpaca", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 4.28, "n_chunks": 5, "n_concurrent": 5, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1178, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": null, "query": "I am going to make pumpkin pie for the first time. Can you help me?", "transcript": "I am going to make pumpkin pie for the first time, can you help me?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Of course I can help you with that. What would you like me to do?", "answer_ms": 596}
